{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975792,"nonce":"YGslcB8x1SOvSSjGtDIOm2aX/Tk=","title":"CI cannot answer 'did this commit help?'","message":"Every benchmark is its own matrix job on its own runner, so a sweep is nine or ten\nmachines drawn from a heterogeneous pool -- three distinct CPU models in a single\nsweep is normal. Absolute milliseconds are therefore not comparable across\nsweeps, and no ratio rescues it in general: a ratio between two FROZEN engines\nstill moves up to 2.03x across runs.\n\nWithin a row the ratios ARE sound, since ash and every reference engine are timed\nin the same job on the same VM.\n\nThe fix that would actually answer the question: an in-sweep A/B. Build HEAD^ (or\na named ref) alongside HEAD in the existing build job, ship it as `ash-base` in\nthe toolchain artifact, and time both interleaved in the same leg on the same VM.\nRoughly +2 minutes of sweep wall time, no new jobs. Until that exists, a local\ninterleaved A/B is the only trustworthy verdict on a performance change.","files":null}]}